I found several existing uses of **Everward**, including one exact book title, one story/location name, and some software/brand uses.

- **Book:** *EVERWARD: One Man's Journey Through Declaration, Discovery & Discernment* by W.G. Kirst. Goodreads lists it as a published work. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="0"}
- **Writing/poetry brand:** Bill Kirst also uses **EVERWARD** as the name of a Substack/project and describes his first published work as a poetry collection titled *EVERWARD*. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="1"}
- **Fantasy fiction:** In the current Royal Road story *Hycadia*, **Everward** is the name of the village where the story begins, and Chapter 1 itself is titled “Everward.” :chatgpt-content-reference{index="2"}
- **Apps/software:** There is an iOS productivity app called **Everward by AInovations**, plus current projects using Everward for fitness and quantitative-trading software. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="3"}
- **Other fiction:** *The Carmine Crusade* uses **Everward** as the name of an imperial capital. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="4"}

What I **didn't** find in this search is a prominent, established commercial video game whose main title is simply **Everward**. The name is therefore not heavily associated with one famous game, but it is definitely **not an unused coined word**.

The existing uses also reveal something interesting about how the word is naturally interpreted. People independently use it for ideas involving **journey, progress, continuation, distance, growth, and moving onward**. That supports the meaning we were reading into it rather than contradicting it.

For your game, the main collision I would care about is the published **EVERWARD** literary title/project. It does not automatically make the game name unusable, but it means **Everward is already an established creative title**, not a blank piece of linguistic territory.
